 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Supplies",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Runs from a single 5V supply or from ±5V. The negative supply draws 3mA quiescent, so the V– pin can be fed from almost any negative voltage through a resistive divider, and the part's performance does not change with the negative supply voltage. Electrical figures apply only up to a 5.4V positive supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output stage",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Q OUT and Q̄ OUT are matched complementary outputs that drive actively both up and down, into TTL/CMOS inputs or passive loads, without the large supply current spikes of a conventional output stage. The part stays stable with its outputs in the linear region, so a slow or small input causes little output glitching, and switching hardly changes the supply current. Each output is a sink-source pair able to drive capacitance such as a feedforward capacitor (Figure A1); unloaded it swings from 0V to typically 3V to 4V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Common mode operation",
