   "page": 6,
   "text": "Bias current figures are taken with the output held at 1.4V. The input pair is NPN, so the current into a low input falls to zero and into a high input doubles; with both inputs less than 0.8V above V– both currents are zero. Above the positive common mode limit the bias current climbs quickly, to several milliamperes at VIN = V+. Differential input resistance is about 10kΩ at zero differential input and rises fast with larger DC differential inputs; common mode input resistance is about 4MΩ at zero differential input. With a large differential input the high input shows about 2MΩ and the low input more than 20MΩ. Input capacitance is 3.5pF typical, found from the change in propagation delay with 1k in series with the input."
  },
  {
