   "page": 6,
   "text": "Taking LATCH ENABLE high holds the input decision at the outputs. Left unconnected the pin floats high, so it is grounded for flow-through operation. To be captured, the input must be valid 5ns before the latch goes high (setup) and remain valid 3ns after (hold). When the latch goes low again, new data reaches the outputs in about 8ns to 10ns. The pin is meant for TTL or CMOS gate drive and has no hysteresis. Below its threshold, current flows out of the pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Measuring response time",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Gain-bandwidth is about 50GHz. The usual test is a 100mV step with 5mV overdrive, which needs a source settling to within 1% (1mV) in a few nanoseconds without ringing or tailing; ordinary pulse generators cannot do this. Figure 1 makes such a step with a 2N3866 in common base: the generator's edge turns the transistor off and its collector settles at 0V in a few nanoseconds, with no feedthrough to the comparator input. V1 is set so the output under test sits at 1.4V, then changed by –5V for 5mV overdrive. The circuit tests a low-to-high step on +IN; swapping the inputs tests the other polarity. In Figure 1 the total lead length of each supply bypass capacitor, device pin and socket included, is under 0.5 inch, over a ground plane."
