   "page": 11,
   "text": "A large capacitive load makes slow, broken transitions; heavy capacitive loads are buffered. A long output lead acts as an unterminated transmission line and rings. Output leads are kept short, and beyond a few inches are terminated with a resistor, typically 250Ω to 400Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Level shifting",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Level-shift stages for the TTL-level output need fast transistors: nanosecond switching and fT close to 1GHz. Figure A2: the output drives a 2N2369 whose base-emitter voltages reverse as the comparator switches, followed by a 2N3866 emitter follower and a Schottky diode that helps sinking, for a 15V output. Figure A3: Q1, a gated current source, switches the Baker-clamped Q2 through a large feedforward capacitor, giving 3ns delay on both edges and a swing that can sit anywhere between 15V and –15V; it suits FET switch gate drive. Figure A5 replaces the Schottky diode of A2 with a sink transistor; its two emitter followers drive a power MOSFET switching 1A at 15V, with 7ns to 9ns delay, mostly in the MOSFET and the 2N2369."
